ready: 1
kind: sequence
id: memory.seq.write
display_id: SEQ-MEMORY-WRITE
module: memory
title: Write a memory
summary: >-
  Checks, tiers, and gates a memory, then stores it with its version, provenance, and change
  record in one commit.
notes: >-
  When the content is a repeat, the existing memory is returned instead.
trigger:
  trigger_kind: call
  text: An agent, the owner, processing, sleep, import, or transcript capture writes a memory of any kind
operation: memory.op.episode
participants:
  - id: caller
    is: [sys.python, sys.cli]
    label: Python API, CLI command, or MCP tool
  - id: core
    is: [sys.daemon]
    label: Kernle core
    component: memory
  - id: db
    is: [sys.home]
    label: Stack database
items: []
parts:
  - memory.seq.write.a
  - memory.seq.write.b
rationale: >-
  Every kind goes through this one path so trust, the input gate, deduplication, provenance, and
  the change ledger apply the same way to an episode as to a value. Earlier designs let each kind
  write its own table, and the layers drifted apart.


  The core is the daemon when it runs, because the daemon is the only writer of stack databases
  while it runs. When it is stopped, the Python package or CLI opens the stack itself under the
  stack's write lock, so writes never fail just because the daemon is down.
